<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<!--
	Deliberately empty.

	Vespa refuses deploys that would silently destroy data — dropping a field,
	changing an attribute's type, removing a document type — and the refusal is
	the feature. An override belongs here only when somebody has decided the
	data loss is intended, with an `until` date that forces the entry to expire
	rather than becoming permanent:

		<allow until="2026-09-01" comment="dropping unused freshness attribute">field-type-change</allow>

	If a deploy fails with a validation id, read what it is protecting before
	adding it here. On this application the likely one is `indexing-change`,
	which means every document has to be re-fed to be correct again.
-->
<validation-overrides>
	<!--
		Moving embedding generation out of Vespa and into the Embedding Worker
		(ADR-001) changes how these fields are produced, so Vespa correctly warns
		that every existing document's vectors are now stale.

		That is true and intended: the worker re-embeds everything it finds with a
		mismatched hash, which is the same path an embedding-model upgrade takes.
		Building it here means the upgrade path is proven before it is needed.

		Expires deliberately. If this entry is still here after the date, either
		the re-index never ran or somebody is using it to wave through a different
		change.
	-->
	<allow until="2026-09-15" comment="ADR-001: embeddings move to the worker; full re-index follows">indexing-change</allow>

	<!--
		The capability vector also changes shape. It was a multi-vector over
		invented intent phrases, `tensor(p{},x[384])`; it is now one vector per
		capability over the specification's own fields. The phrases are gone with
		the intent field, so there is nothing left to have several vectors of.
	-->
	<allow until="2026-09-15" comment="ADR-001: capability embedding is one vector, not one per invented phrase">field-type-change</allow>
</validation-overrides>
